WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.241 --> 00:00:01.983
Standing here with Effie

2
00:00:01.983 --> 00:00:03.097
and Ze'ev.
Yes.

3
00:00:03.097 --> 00:00:05.733
We are here on the Golan Heights,

4
00:00:05.733 --> 00:00:08.461
and we are looking into Syria,

5
00:00:08.461 --> 00:00:11.662
sight of just the worst civil war imaginable.

6
00:00:11.662 --> 00:00:13.662
Syrians, who are wounded

7
00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:17.096
or in need of medical attention,

8
00:00:17.096 --> 00:00:21.177
have been getting medical attention from the Israelis.

9
00:00:21.177 --> 00:00:22.910
Sometimes they are wounded

10
00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:26.219
and sometimes those are sick women or children.

11
00:00:26.219 --> 00:00:27.914
I cannot imagine how they feel.

12
00:00:27.914 --> 00:00:29.400
You know sometimes they come

13
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.431
and they're just so scared,

14
00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:35.577
and you can barely get them talk.

15
00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:37.016
And sometimes they say, "Toda,"

16
00:00:37.016 --> 00:00:38.758
"Thank you" in Hebrew that's,

17
00:00:38.758 --> 00:00:40.244
in my language.
For a Syrian to thank you

18
00:00:40.244 --> 00:00:43.077
in Hebrew is pretty special.
Yes.

19
00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:46.542
You can hear?

20
00:00:46.542 --> 00:00:48.103
That's the civil war.

21
00:00:48.103 --> 00:00:49.186
What we're hearing right now--

22
00:00:49.186 --> 00:00:50.442
Right now is exchange of fire

23
00:00:50.442 --> 00:00:52.149
between the regime and the rebels.

24
00:00:52.149 --> 00:00:54.459
That's the city of Nakonechna.

25
00:00:54.459 --> 00:00:56.897
That city is controlled by the regime.

26
00:00:56.897 --> 00:00:58.975
Okay? By Assad's military.

27
00:00:58.975 --> 00:01:02.322
And the village right here is controlled by rebels.

28
00:01:02.322 --> 00:01:03.851
That's machine gun fire we hear,

29
00:01:03.851 --> 00:01:04.684
rifle fire--
Machine guns,

30
00:01:04.684 --> 00:01:05.554
heavy machine guns.

31
00:01:05.554 --> 00:01:07.612
[Conan] Heavy machine guns.

32
00:01:07.612 --> 00:01:10.143
(bellowing gunfire)

33
00:01:10.143 --> 00:01:12.204
Now, this is going on all the time.

34
00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:14.247
Uh, this is relatively calm, okay?

35
00:01:14.247 --> 00:01:16.442
Relatively calm, relative to war.

36
00:01:16.442 --> 00:01:17.928
That's mind-numbing that

37
00:01:17.928 --> 00:01:19.867
that's relatively calm.
That's relatively calm.

38
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:23.849
Yeah, I've never heard an actual war before.

39
00:01:23.849 --> 00:01:25.766
That's pretty sobering.

40
00:01:27.498 --> 00:01:29.646 line:15%
Syrians are treated near the border,

41
00:01:29.646 --> 00:01:31.504 line:15%
and then they're taken here.

42
00:01:31.504 --> 00:01:35.451 line:15%
Standing here with Dr. Roisentul and with Fares.

43
00:01:35.451 --> 00:01:39.050 line:15%
Dr. Roisentul, obviously you treat

44
00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.289
the Syrians that come here and need help.

45
00:01:42.289 --> 00:01:44.786
And, Fares, you are a social worker.

46
00:01:44.786 --> 00:01:47.293
You speak Arabic and you say that's important

47
00:01:47.293 --> 00:01:50.033
because when the Syrians come in they're very afraid,

48
00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:51.984
so if someone can speak Arabic to them,

49
00:01:51.984 --> 00:01:52.962
it calms them, is that right?

50
00:01:52.962 --> 00:01:53.795
Yes.

51
00:01:53.795 --> 00:01:57.848
You're treating Syrians here who've been injured

52
00:01:57.848 --> 00:01:59.253
in the civil war.

53
00:01:59.253 --> 00:02:00.541
You don't treat them just medically,

54
00:02:00.541 --> 00:02:02.794
they're also treated psychologically, is that true?

55
00:02:02.794 --> 00:02:03.627
Yeah, that true,

56
00:02:03.627 --> 00:02:06.291
because some people they have illnesses.

57
00:02:06.291 --> 00:02:08.665
They also come here because they have a trauma.

58
00:02:08.665 --> 00:02:09.498
[Conan] Right.

59
00:02:09.498 --> 00:02:10.819
They have a trauma from the war.

60
00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:13.768
They have a trauma from leaving the country.

61
00:02:13.768 --> 00:02:15.800
I think you're doing God's work here.

62
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.564
I honestly, sincerely think you're doing

63
00:02:17.564 --> 00:02:18.876
beautiful work here.

64
00:02:18.876 --> 00:02:20.583
The only thing I question about you

65
00:02:20.583 --> 00:02:22.022
are these medical Crocs.

66
00:02:22.022 --> 00:02:22.905
(crowd laughing)
That, I think,

67
00:02:22.905 --> 00:02:25.633
is the only thing that separates you

68
00:02:25.633 --> 00:02:28.729
from a godlike status.
(crowd laughing)

69
00:02:28.729 --> 00:02:30.017
We are only human.

70
00:02:30.017 --> 00:02:32.168
[Conan] Yeah, we all have our mistakes.

71
00:02:32.168 --> 00:02:33.479
You bought those.
(crowd laughing)

72
00:02:33.479 --> 00:02:35.951
You saw them at the mall and you had to have them.

73
00:02:35.951 --> 00:02:37.312
Other than the Crocs,

74
00:02:37.312 --> 00:02:38.531
I think you should get the Peace Prize,

75
00:02:38.531 --> 00:02:39.364
I really do.

76
00:02:39.364 --> 00:02:40.280
(clapping)
Next I visited

77
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.158
with some of the Syrian patients,

78
00:02:42.158 --> 00:02:43.549
whose faces we had to blur

79
00:02:43.549 --> 00:02:44.757 line:15%
for their own protection.

80
00:02:44.757 --> 00:02:48.674 line:15%
(speaking in foreign language)

81
00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.240
Please tell him that I'm very sorry

82
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.958
for his injury and his pain.

83
00:02:55.958 --> 00:02:59.047
And, coming here and seeing everyone here

84
00:02:59.047 --> 00:03:01.241
who's been injured and meeting them,

85
00:03:01.241 --> 00:03:03.586
it's very powerful for me.

86
00:03:03.586 --> 00:03:07.557
The world is very aware of the horrors in Syria.

87
00:03:07.557 --> 00:03:10.261
And, we're hoping that together,

88
00:03:10.261 --> 00:03:14.428
maybe we can find a solution so that this can stop.

